; Failing on purpose.
;
; Until this module there were two ways out of a program that had gone wrong: a
; trap the program did not ask for, and `std:process:exit`, which says nothing.
; Neither is what a function does when its caller broke the contract.
;
; It is `core` because the failure path is (`D-080`): a freestanding program
; supplies `sl_rt_panic` and gets the same behaviour a hosted one gets, and
; `scripts/core-check.sh` links a `-nostdlib` program that calls into here.
;
; A panic exits with status 101, which is what every deliberate failure in this
; language exits with — an overflow, an index past the end, a division by zero
; (`D-031`). There is no way to catch one: a `Result` is how a failure a caller
; can answer is carried (`D-087`), and this is the other kind.

(export panic assert unreachable)

; The message crosses as the pointer alone, NUL-terminated, which is what a
; borrowed `String` is at the boundary (`D-065`).
(extern "sl_rt_panic" (panic-raw (message &String)) -> unit)

; The result is `unit` rather than a type that says "never", because this
; language has no such type and adding one to spell three functions would be a
; larger change than the functions (`D-130`). So a panic is written where a
; statement goes — under a `when`, at the end of a branch — and not where a
; value is expected.
(fn panic ((message &String)) -> unit
  (panic-raw message))

(fn assert ((held bool) (message &String)) -> unit
  (when (not held)
    (panic message)))

(fn unreachable () -> unit
  (panic &"reached a branch the program said it could not"))
